; `.cbor` applied to a head that is not `bytes`. RFC 8610 §3.8.4 restricts `.cbor` to byte strings —
; its payload IS the content of those bytes — so refusing the shape is right; refusing it with a
; `panic!` was not, and it now refuses gracefully naming the head. Name-INDEPENDENT (the same
; refusal fires for every non-bytes head, supported names included), so like `rangeop_typename_start`
; it is a SHAPE-axis row and the refused-name closure sweep (`refused_name_closure_tests`) keeps this
; column `ContextOwned` with a `Refuses` verdict. The MEMBER route is its sibling row
; (`tests/robustness/ctl_cbor_non_bytes_head_member.cddl`).
x = uint .cbor uint
